  "notes": "The West Coast record that sounds least like g-funk while still being West Coast, and the research is explicit about why: the drums are \"the priority layer\". So this is the fastest entry of the LA group (94–104), the loudest and least quantized kit (`quantizeStrength` 0.76, `velocityVar` 0.22, `percs.gainOffsetDb` -3), the heaviest ghost-note 16ths (`ghost.prob` 0.42 across all four e/a slots) and the busiest hats (`fillDensity` 0.62). Everything melodic is deliberately thin against it: one horn or keys stab, `densityPerBar` [2,5], and harmony described as \"incidental\" — hence `impliedByRiff` and a two-family chord set. The bass is jazz-funk upright walking rather than sub, which is why its register floor is 31 — the highest bass in this batch, because the research says the low end is drums-first. ⚠ `medium` on the research's medium-high rating. The INFERRED parameters are the chord families and `chordDurationBeats` [2,10]: \"static single-chord vamps\" is all the research states, so the i–iv family is inference carrying the chord part's variety floor, and it is the first thing to delete. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 50 to 49 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 50 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
